\b\fs28 (f) DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF THE INVENTION\b0\fs22\par
\b\fs24 1. The Client-Side Pose Pipeline\b0\fs22\par
[Detailed description of the per-frame pipeline as in Section III.B of the research paper. Refer to Figure 1.]\par\par
\b\fs24 2. The Rule Grammar (Claim 1 / Claim 6 basis)\b0\fs22\par
A pose `P` is characterised by:\par\par
\f1\fs20 P = (N, S, R, T)\par     N \u2014 Sanskrit name\par     S \u2014 stance class \u2208 \{stand, seat, prone, supine\}\par     R \u2014 finite rule set \{r_1, ..., r_k\}\par     T \u2014 target hold duration (seconds)\par \par r_i = (\u03c6_i, \u03c4_i, c_i)\par     \u03c6_i \u2014 predicate over smoothed keypoints\par     \u03c4_i \u2014 confidence threshold\par     c_i \u2014 natural-language correction string\par \par \u03c6 ::= angle(K_a, K_b, K_c) \u2208 [\u03b8_min, \u03b8_max]\par     | dist(K_a, K_b) / dist(K_c, K_d) \u2208 [\u03c1_min, \u03c1_max]\f0\fs22\par\par
Twenty curated poses are encoded in this grammar; the complete rule sets are in Annexure A.\par\par
\b\fs24 3. The Hysteresis Filter (Independent Claim 1)\b0\fs22\par
The state machine for each rule i:\par\par
\f1\fs20 state[i]      \u2208 \{silent, speaking\}    initially silent\par fail_cnt[i]   \u2208 \u2115                     initially 0\par pass_cnt[i]   \u2208 \u2115                     initially 0\par F             = 7   (fail threshold)\par P             = 5   (pass threshold)\f0\fs22\par\par
On each frame at time t:\par\par
\f1\fs20 if NOT \u03c6_i(p\u0302_t):                      // rule failing\par     fail_cnt[i] := fail_cnt[i] + 1\par     pass_cnt[i] := 0\par     if state[i] = silent and fail_cnt[i] \u2265 F:\par         emit_speech(c_i)\par         state[i] := speaking\par else:                                  // rule passing\par     pass_cnt[i] := pass_cnt[i] + 1\par     fail_cnt[i] := 0\par     if state[i] = speaking and pass_cnt[i] \u2265 P:\par         state[i] := silent\f0\fs22\par\par
The asymmetry F > P is intentional: the system enters the speaking state cautiously (requires 280 ms of failure at 25 fps) but exits it quickly (requires 200 ms of passing) \u2014 rewarding the user's correction with rapid silence.\par\par
\b\fs24 4. The Voice Command Grammar (Independent Claim 2)\b0\fs22\par
\f1\fs20 RESOLVE(utterance):\par     for each (regex_j, handler_j) in PRIORITY_TABLE:\par         if regex_j.test(utterance):\par             return handler_j(utterance)\par     pose_name := fuzzy_match(utterance, ASANA_LIBRARY)\par     if pose_name is not null:\par         return select_pose(pose_name)\par     return error_unrecognised\f0\fs22\par\par
Where PRIORITY_TABLE is a static ordered list of 12 (regex, handler) pairs covering: end session, score query, next/previous, reference overlay, repeat, voice toggle, breath toggle, diagnostics, hold timer, and pose modifications. The fallback fuzzy match uses a normalised Levenshtein distance against the canonical Sanskrit and English aliases of the 20 poses in the library.\par\par
\b\fs24 5. The Multi-Tenant Data Layer (Independent Claim 3)\b0\fs22\par
Each request to a protected route passes through a decorator that:\par\par
\f1\fs20 def require_org(handler):\par     def wrapper(request):\par         user = authenticate(request)\par         if user.role not in \{"org_admin", "student"\}: deny()\par         request._org_email := user.organizationEmail\par         return handler(request)\par     return wrapper\f0\fs22\par\par
All MongoDB queries within `handler` then filter by `\{ organizationEmail: request._org_email \}`. There is no code path in the application by which an org_admin can query data outside their `organizationEmail` scope.\par\par
\b\fs24 6. The Certification Module (Independent Claim 4)\b0\fs22\par
A pose `P` is *passed* by user `u` when:\par\par
\f1\fs20 \u2203 session s: \par     s.user = u, s.pose = P, max_duration_at(score \u2265 90%, s) \u2265 T_P\par \par AND\par \par \u2203 quiz_attempt q:\par     q.user = u, q.pose = P, q.score = 100%\f0\fs22\par\par
A semester is *passed* when all its poses are passed. The master certificate is *issued* when all six semesters are passed; the certificate is a PDF signed with an RSA-2048 key whose public half is published at a verification URL embedded in the PDF.\par\par
\b\fs28 (g) CLAIMS\b0\fs22\par
We claim:\par\par
**1.** A method for real-time yoga pose coaching, comprising:\par\par
(a) capturing a video stream from a user-facing camera of a client device;\par\par
(b) executing a single-person convolutional pose-estimation neural network within a web browser running on the said client device, said neural network producing a set of keypoint coordinates and confidence scores per frame;\par\par
(c) smoothing said keypoint coordinates by an exponential moving average to obtain smoothed keypoints;\par\par
(d) evaluating, against the said smoothed keypoints, a finite set of pose-specific rule predicates each comprising either an angle constraint over three keypoints or a distance-ratio constraint over two pairs of keypoints;\par\par
(e) maintaining, for each said rule predicate, a fail counter and a pass counter, and a state flag taking values in \{silent, speaking\};\par\par
(f) emitting a corresponding pre-defined natural-language correction string to a speech synthesis subsystem if and only if the said state flag for the said rule predicate is silent AND the said fail counter exceeds a fail threshold;\par\par
(g) transitioning the said state flag from speaking back to silent if and only if the said pass counter exceeds a pass threshold;\par\par
characterised in that the said fail threshold is strictly greater than the said pass threshold, and the said keypoint inference of step (b) is performed entirely on the said client device with no raw video, image, or keypoint data transmitted to any remote server.\par\par
**2.** A method for resolving voice commands during yoga practice, comprising:\par\par
(a) receiving a recognised speech utterance from a speech- recognition subsystem of a web browser;\par\par
(b) testing the said utterance against a priority-ordered list of regular expressions each mapped to a respective action handler;\par\par
(c) invoking the action handler corresponding to the first said regular expression that matches the said utterance;\par\par
(d) and only if no said regular expression matches, performing a fuzzy lexical match of the said utterance against a catalogue of pose names and invoking a pose-selection action;\par\par
characterised in that the said priority-ordered list disambiguates utterances containing tokens that are simultaneously valid as action keywords and as pose-name components by deterministically resolving in favour of the action.\par\par
**3.** A multi-tenant deployment system for the method of claim 1, comprising:\par\par
(a) a server-side data store partitioned by an organisational email-domain identifier;\par\par
(b) an authentication subsystem that associates each authenticated user with exactly one said organisational identifier;\par\par
(c) an access-control decorator applied to each protected route handler that injects the said organisational identifier into database query filters,\par\par
characterised in that no application code path permits a request authenticated under one said organisational identifier to read or modify data associated with a different said organisational identifier.\par\par
**4.** A certification method for use with the method of claim 1, comprising:\par\par
(a) recording, for each pose-practice session, the maximum duration during which the said alignment score (computed as the fraction of said rule predicates passing) remained at or above ninety percent;\par\par
(b) recording, for each said pose, the score on a fixed-question anatomical quiz;\par\par
(c) marking the said pose as passed when the said maximum duration at or above ninety percent is at least ten seconds AND the said quiz score is one hundred percent;\par\par
(d) marking a semester as passed when all poses assigned to the said semester are marked passed;\par\par
(e) issuing a cryptographically signed PDF certificate when all six semesters of a fixed curriculum are marked passed.\par\par
**5.** The method of claim 1 wherein the said exponential moving average smoothing parameter is in the range [0.5, 0.7] and the said pose-estimation neural network is MoveNet SinglePose Thunder.\par\par
**6.** The method of claim 1 wherein the said fail threshold is seven frames and the said pass threshold is five frames at a nominal frame rate of twenty-five frames per second.\par\par
**7.** The method of claim 1 wherein the said rule predicates for asymmetric poses are parameterised by a leading-side flag and the said voice command grammar of claim 2 accepts said leading-side as an optional suffix to the said pose name.\par\par
**8.** The system of claim 3 further comprising a bulk-onboarding subsystem that ingests an Excel spreadsheet of student records, validates each record against the said organisational domain, generates a single-use invitation token per record, and dispatches a magic-link email containing the said token.\par\par
**9.** A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions which, when executed by one or more processors of a client device having a camera and a microphone, cause the said processors to perform the method of claim 1.\par\par
**10.** A system comprising one or more client devices and a server, configured to perform the method of claim 1 in combination with the multi-tenant deployment system of claim 3 and the certification method of claim 4.\par\par
\b\fs28 (h) ABSTRACT OF THE DISCLOSURE\b0\fs22\par
Disclosed is a browser-native system and method for real-time yoga pose coaching. A web-based client device captures camera video, executes an on-device convolutional pose-estimation neural network, and evaluates the detected keypoints against per-pose rule predicates. A novel hysteresis filter \u2014 a per-rule fail/pass debounce state machine with asymmetric thresholds (fail strictly greater than pass) \u2014 suppresses frame-level feedback chatter and emits spoken corrections only after a sustained failure is observed, returning to silence only after a sustained recovery. A priority-ordered voice command grammar deterministically disambiguates action commands from content references. A server-side multi-tenant data layer partitions student records by organisational email domain with route-level access enforcement. A certification module integrates pose practice and quiz performance to issue cryptographically signed completion certificates for a fixed six-semester twenty-pose curriculum. No raw video, image, or keypoint data is transmitted from the client device.\par\par
